{"id":"tur-t0-0001","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.hello","domain":1,"text_native":"Merhaba.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Hello.","source_caption":"Hello.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The default greeting anywhere. Works with every age and every status.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Turkish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0002","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.morning","domain":1,"text_native":"Günaydın.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Good morning.","source_caption":"Good morning.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Used until roughly midday. Getting the time of day wrong is noticed; not saying it is only noticed by someone expecting it.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Turkish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0003","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.afternoon","domain":1,"text_native":"Tünaydın.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Good afternoon.","source_caption":"Good afternoon.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The middle of the day. In several languages this is the same word as the evening greeting, and using it late is not an error.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Turkish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0004","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.evening","domain":1,"text_native":"İyi akşamlar.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Good evening.","source_caption":"Good evening.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"From late afternoon. Often interchangeable with the afternoon greeting.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Turkish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0005","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.how-are-you","domain":1,"text_native":"Nasılsınız?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"How are you?","source_caption":"How are you?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The opening move in almost every conversation. In most of these languages it is a greeting rather than a question and does not expect a literal answer.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Turkish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0006","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.i-am-well","domain":1,"text_native":"Teşekkürler, iyiyim.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"I am well, thank you.","source_caption":"Fine, thank you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The expected answer to the greeting above. Answering it wrong reads as evasive in cultures where it is a formula.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Turkish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0007","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.nice-to-meet-you","domain":1,"text_native":"Memnun oldum.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Nice to meet you.","source_caption":"Nice to meet you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Said on a first introduction. In several languages there is a separate phrase for meeting someone formally that a stranger will use instead.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Turkish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0008","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.what-is-your-name","domain":1,"text_native":"Adınız nedir?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"What is your name?","source_caption":"What is your name?","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"You get asked this before you get to ask it. Recognising the question is the difference between answering and freezing.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Turkish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0009","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.thank-you","domain":1,"text_native":"Teşekkür ederim.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Thank you.","source_caption":"Thank you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The single most useful word in any of these languages. Saying it costs nothing and changes how everything after it is received.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Turkish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0010","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.youre-welcome","domain":1,"text_native":"Bir şey değil.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"You are welcome.","source_caption":"You're welcome.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"You will be handed it constantly, and there is no way to avoid replying. In a few languages the reply is the same word as 'yes'.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Turkish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0011","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.excuse-me","domain":1,"text_native":"Bakar mısınız?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Excuse me.","source_caption":"Excuse me. (getting attention)","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Two different things in one phrase: getting someone's attention, and getting past them. Guessing wrong is jarring — one version walks through a conversation.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Turkish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0012","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.sorry","domain":1,"text_native":"Özür dilerim.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"I am sorry.","source_caption":"I'm sorry.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Apologising for a small fault — bumping someone, being late, not understanding. The form you use signals how much of a fault you think it was.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Turkish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0013","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.goodbye","domain":1,"text_native":"Hoşçakalın.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Goodbye.","source_caption":"Goodbye","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"How you leave. Leaving without one is noticeably rude in most of these languages.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Turkish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0014","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"repair.dont-understand","domain":12,"text_native":"Anlamıyorum","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"I do not understand.","source_caption":"I don't understand.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The one phrase that keeps a conversation alive. Without it people stop trying, and with it they often rephrase in simpler words.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Turkish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0015","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"repair.cannot-hear","domain":12,"text_native":"Onu tekrar söyle lütfen.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"I cannot hear you.","source_caption":"Say that again, please.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Distinguishes not hearing from not understanding, because they have different fixes and conflating them produces the wrong one.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"attested","id":"Tatoeba tur-en sentence pairs (CC BY 2.0 FR)","url":"https://tatoeba.org/en/downloads","licence":"CC BY 2.0 FR, some CC0","external_id":"5032180","author":"CM"},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0016","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"repair.how-do-you-say","domain":12,"text_native":"Fransızcada nasıl \"teşekkürler\" diyorsunuz?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"How do you say this?","source_caption":"How do you say \"thanks\" in French?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"How you learn a word instead of nodding. Asking is how a phrasebook gets used instead of recited.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"attested","id":"Tatoeba tur-en sentence pairs (CC BY 2.0 FR)","url":"https://tatoeba.org/en/downloads","licence":"CC BY 2.0 FR, some CC0","external_id":"4858858","author":"PvtMarc"},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0017","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"bridge.understand","domain":12,"text_native":"Anlıyor musun?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Do you understand?","source_caption":"Do you understand?","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"You get asked it after anything you say, and the honest answer is the only useful one.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"attested","id":"Tatoeba tur-en sentence pairs (CC BY 2.0 FR)","url":"https://tatoeba.org/en/downloads","licence":"CC BY 2.0 FR, some CC0","external_id":"593228","author":"Ramses"},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"tur-t0-0018","lang":"tur","tier":0,"slot":"bridge.speak-english","domain":12,"text_native":"İngilizce biliyor musunuz?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Do you speak English?","source_caption":"Do you speak English?","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"Asked of you constantly, and the question that decides whether anything after it can happen. There is a polite version and a blunt version and the gap between them is wide.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Turkish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
